00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.500
and the really interesting
stuff that you get up to,

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.110
can you tell us a little
bit about who you are

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:01.940
and what you do and the
company you work for?

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.273
CRAIG GANSSLE: Sure, absolutely.

00:05:03.273 --> 00:05:04.740
I am Craig Ganssle, as you said.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.970
I am the founder and CEO
of Basecamp Networks.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:11.900
And Basecamp Networks is a
innovative technology company

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:17.450
that works with wireless
infrastructures and software

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.940
development using
artificial intelligence

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:22.790
and machine learning,
specifically in agriculture.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.225
But we do work in some verticals
outside of agriculture.

00:05:25.225 --> 00:05:27.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What were
you doing at Cloud Next?

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:28.940
How do you use the cloud?

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.330
You're talking
about agriculture,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.280
you're talking about plants.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.050
What is the technological
part of this?

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:33.883
CRAIG GANSSLE: Sure.

00:05:33.883 --> 00:05:36.260
We built a product in 2013--

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.660
an application
called intelliSCOUT.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:41.510
And intelliSCOUT is an
artificial intelligence

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:44.240
platform that uses
image recognition

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.350
to detect pathogens in plants
on crops and agriculture

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:49.490
in real time.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:52.370
The entire thing lives in
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.340
And we love it living there.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.870
It has had great results.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.170
And being that it's
in the cloud like that

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.330
allows us to scale
dramatically around the world.

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:04.460
intelliSCOUT is being
used in multiple countries

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:05.450
around the world.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.120
And it's used highly
in R&amp;D environments

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.470
with large corporations to
do better trialing and better

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.850
production for seeds and
the performance of crops,

00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:17.840
I guess we could say.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.881
When seeds go in the ground
and we plant and then

00:06:20.881 --> 00:06:22.505
everything grows in
the middle and then

00:06:22.505 --> 00:06:23.750
we harvest in the end--

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:25.850
when everything's
growing in the middle,

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:28.250
people are trying to
capture data from the field

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.470
before we could potentially
have a crop that

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.320
gets affected by a disease
or we lose that crop.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.440
And so intelliSCOUT takes
the actionable insights

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:41.170
from the field that would
normally take weeks or a month

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.960
even to diagnose and get back
in the hands of a farmer.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.959
With intelliSCOUT, it
happens in minutes.

00:06:46.959 --> 00:06:48.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
some time ago, we

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.600
interviewed Descartes Labs.

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.550
And they do
something really cool

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.310
where they're also
be able to identify

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.530
some problems like
forests and stuff

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.660
like that through
satellite images.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.100
Is that how you do it?

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.720
What is the input
to your application?

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.140
CRAIG GANSSLE: So we don't
do anything with satellite

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:07.070
right now.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.980
What we do is we
use mobile devices--

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:11.750
what people are already using--

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:14.300
and actually as well
with Google Glass, which

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:17.720
is how my entire vision
for intelliSCOUT began.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:21.800
I was one of the early people
asked to come up to New York

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:23.062
and get Glass.

00:07:23.062 --> 00:07:24.770
And that's really kind
of how it started.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:29.090
So Google Glass, iOS right
now, coming soon on Android,

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.554
and also soon on drones.

00:07:30.554 --> 00:07:31.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.380
So you're one of those
Google Glass explorers?

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:33.730
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The pioneers?

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:36.254
Nice.

00:07:36.254 --> 00:07:36.920
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.545
So just so I get this
right in my head,

00:07:38.545 --> 00:07:40.490
so basically you have
people out in the field,

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.920
either using their mobile
phones or Google Glass,

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.740
taking photos of crops
and then sending that back

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:48.570
to some services that run
in the cloud or Google Cloud

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.740
Platform that then process
that and determine information

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:55.190
about it, such as yield or
disease or other such things.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.360
Is that pretty much correct?

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.151
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah,
that's pretty much it.

00:07:58.151 --> 00:08:00.820
It has some additional
automations.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.740
In crop monitoring,
there are some things--

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.830
like, with corn, they will count
the kernels on an ear of corn

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:08.210
to help predict yield.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:11.180
And today, a lot of times
they do that with a Sharpie.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:16.780
Well, if you've ever stood out
in July in a field in Georgia,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:17.720
it gets really hot.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.090
And so what was one
of the first things

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.730
we did with Google Glass is now
you can take an ear of corn,

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.600
hold it up in front of Google
Glass or an iOS phone or tablet

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.970
and snap a picture,
and it counts

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.725
the kernels with over 97%
accuracy in about two seconds.

00:08:31.725 --> 00:08:32.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Wow.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:34.979
That is probably higher
than humans when it's hot.

00:08:34.979 --> 00:08:36.770
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah,
the average percentage

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.510
that people were accepting
in the ag industry

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.767
was about 72% to 86%.

00:08:42.767 --> 00:08:43.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, nice.

00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:44.300
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.924
CRAIG GANSSLE: So
we've increased that,

00:08:45.924 --> 00:08:48.320
and we've narrowed the time
window in which it happens.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:51.560
So it takes the picture, sends
everything up to the cloud,

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.120
runs the process,
comes back down

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:54.770
about two seconds with results.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.340
MARK MANDEL: So
what powers that?

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:58.192
Is that TensorFlow?

00:08:58.192 --> 00:08:59.150
Is that something else?

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:00.800
Is that some kind of
machine learning thing?

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:01.880
What's the deal there?

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:02.713
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah.

00:09:02.713 --> 00:09:08.070
So we started with open CV, and
now we are using TensorFlow.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.570
There's a lot of components
that we're doing--

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:15.519
Firebase, I think we're
using Pub/Sub and Kubernetes.

00:09:15.519 --> 00:09:17.060
We're using a lot
of the Google Cloud

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.144
Platform-- the full stack.

00:09:18.144 --> 00:09:19.934
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
let's concentrate first

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:22.490
on the part that, for me, is
the most exciting one, which

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.000
is the machine
learning side of it.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.400
You say that you
started with open CV.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:30.620
Could you tell a bit
to the audience that

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.630
doesn't know what open CV is?

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.490
And why were you using it?

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.417
And what made you
migrate to TensorFlow?

00:09:36.417 --> 00:09:37.250
CRAIG GANSSLE: Sure.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:41.140
I will answer that best as I
can without my chief developer.

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:45.530
But open CV is open
Computer Vision.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:51.770
And that was a bit more of an
open source sort of practice

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:54.920
that we just started
messing around with.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:59.150
TensorFlow-- it was just faster,
The way the algorithms sort of

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.410
compared the data
that was coming in.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.460
And it was able to
train the machine

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:07.610
to learn the results
quicker, if that makes sense.

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:11.180
So taking a cataloged
library of imagery

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.360
and capturing multiple pictures,
you have to train the machine.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.880
So first, you have to start
with kind of your zero base.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.760
Hey, this is a healthy plant.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.550
And then, this is
corn leaf blight,

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.000
and then this is corn
leaf blight pretty severe.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.060
And this is corn leaf
blight really bad.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:30.770
And TensorFlow managed
to take those images

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:34.780
and sort of process them a
little bit more seamlessly--

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:37.310
that the results became
far more accurate

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:40.280
when we took our
algorithms, mixed them

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.820
with TensorFlow
algorithms, and really

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.969
got the entire sequence running.

00:10:45.969 --> 00:10:48.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think that
it is pretty interesting,

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:51.320
because open CV is not
really machine learning.

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.730
I've used it before.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.907
And it allows you to do things
like counting the kernels

00:10:55.907 --> 00:10:56.990
in the corn, for instance.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.280
It would be able to do that.

00:10:58.280 --> 00:10:58.710
CRAIG GANSSLE: Right.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
you need to write it.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:03.110
Detecting something like
how healthy this plant is,

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.850
that sounds actually
pretty amazing.

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:06.516
MARK MANDEL: I was
going to ask, did you

00:11:06.516 --> 00:11:10.460
have a catalog of imagery of
diseased corn or something

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:11.900
like that to train this against?

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:15.590
Did you have that prepared, or
did you have to go find that?

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:17.090
CRAIG GANSSLE:
That's a funny story.

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:17.600
No.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.580
Actually, the answer
is no, we didn't.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:20.840
And we searched.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:24.030
We searched agriculture
universities,

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:27.350
other universities,
large seed corporations,

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.540
even the Library of Congress.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:33.500
Nobody had properly
tagged images

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.010
of crops or the
pathogens or pests

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.320
that could infect
crops for all the ones

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.880
that we started working on.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.070
So we had to really start
creating them ourselves.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:47.270
And that's been a
very daunting task.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.310
And that is also where
TensorFlow helped.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:54.440
Where we were originally having
to capture thousands of images

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:58.580
to train the machine,
now we can probably

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:03.080
get away with 40 to 60 images to
get the machine trained to have

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:05.450
really good accuracy results.

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.280
But nobody really had anything.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.680
People had images, but they
weren't cataloged properly.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.800
They weren't tagged with, what
is this and what pathogen is it

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:14.234
and what's the severity level.

00:12:14.234 --> 00:12:15.900
And severity level
has been really hard,

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:17.870
because that's
very much dependant

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:19.770
upon the subject matter experts.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:24.050
So one pathologist might
say, hey, this is 30%.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:26.930
Another might say, no, I
think it's more like 45%.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.630
And so a couple of different
countries, including

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:32.270
the USDA and multiple
companies, have

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.580
been looking at
intelliSCOUT to possibly

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:39.350
be that first benchmark to
set a standard in an accuracy

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:39.890
setting--

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:43.670
a benchmark setting in the
industry-- because of the way

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:46.514
it's capturing information
and we're analyzing it.

00:12:46.514 --> 00:12:47.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So something that

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:50.120
really surprised me was
you said that, well,

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:52.760
to train the model,
at the beginning,

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.670
you had to use thousands
of images, which is what

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:58.220
sounds normal to me, really.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.870
When you are doing
deep learning,

00:12:59.870 --> 00:13:01.520
you really need big data.

00:13:01.520 --> 00:13:03.980
But then you say that
you went down to 40, 60.

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:06.440
What magic is this?

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.350
CRAIG GANSSLE: That's some of
the algorithms and the ways

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:12.170
that we internally have come
up with training the machine.

00:13:12.170 --> 00:13:13.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
sounds top secret.

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:15.380
CRAIG GANSSLE: Kind of, yeah.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:18.610
Yeah, that falls under the
Intellectual Property Clause.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.740
It also is very dependent upon
what disease we're looking at.

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:26.430
Some are very easily
detectable, and some are not.

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:28.070
And for the ones that
are not, we still

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:31.010
do sometimes need to
get up in higher images.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.190
But it's really not in
the thousands anymore.

00:13:33.190 --> 00:13:35.450
It can kind of just
be in the hundreds.

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:38.840
We built into the platform,
both in Google Glass

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.139
and on the iOS app,
a reticle design.

00:13:41.139 --> 00:13:43.180
So when you launch the
camera, there's a reticle.

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.380
And when you line up
the leaf or the crop

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:47.540
of the plant in that
reticle, it filters out

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:49.760
a lot of the background
noise automatically.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:52.670
So you're basically certain
to get a good picture

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:53.720
right from the get-go.

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:54.470
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:57.085
So I know nothing
about agriculture.

00:13:57.085 --> 00:13:58.460
But now I'm just
kind of curious,

00:13:58.460 --> 00:13:59.751
because it popped into my head.

00:13:59.751 --> 00:14:01.129
Do you do insects and stuff?

00:14:01.129 --> 00:14:01.670
I don't know.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:02.930
Are locusts still a problem?

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:03.920
I have no idea.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:04.900
Is that a thing?

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:05.733
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah.

00:14:05.733 --> 00:14:08.720
So that falls under the category
of pests, bugs and insects,

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:10.370
and working with entomologists.

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:13.770
Like, in corn, you have
sweet corn armyworm.

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.710
In cotton, you can
have boll weevil--

00:14:15.710 --> 00:14:18.410
different pests that infest the
crop at either an early stage

00:14:18.410 --> 00:14:19.340
or a late stage.

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:20.150
MARK MANDEL: You
talked a little bit

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:22.108
about some of the products
you're using at GCP.

00:14:22.108 --> 00:14:24.750
Can you talk at least
a bit about some

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.000
of the platforms you're using?

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:28.064
You mentioned Kubernetes,
your Firebase.

00:14:28.064 --> 00:14:29.480
Let's stick with
machine learning.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:32.610
Did you use Kubernetes to help
train the TensorFlow models?

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:34.002
Did you use Cloud ML Engine?

00:14:34.002 --> 00:14:34.960
What did you use there?

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:35.793
CRAIG GANSSLE: Yeah.

00:14:35.793 --> 00:14:39.530
So the developers
are using Kubernetes

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:43.160
to satisfy the definitions
automatically with TensorFlow.

00:14:43.160 --> 00:14:46.570
Load balancing, health
checks, and instance groups--

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:47.810
Cloud SQL.

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:51.260
Pub/Sub for production,
depending on some of the system

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:52.260
architecture.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.290
Some of the others, I think I'd
have to get with the developers

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.539
to find out specifically how
they're using everything.

00:14:56.539 --> 00:15:02.120
But yeah, we also are working
with a bit of the Lagom

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.170
framework.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:05.932
But TensorFlow, probably one
of the biggest that we have.

00:15:05.932 --> 00:15:08.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Sorry, what's
the framework you mentioned?

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:09.265
CRAIG GANSSLE: Lagom.

00:15:09.265 --> 00:15:10.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
do not know that.

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:12.516
Could you tell us a
little bit about it?

00:15:12.516 --> 00:15:15.190
CRAIG GANSSLE: It allows
us to sort of push things

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.150
to production in real time.

00:15:17.150 --> 00:15:19.670
MARK MANDEL: So I'll
ask this question then.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.550
So why did you end up
choosing Google Cloud

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:23.410
Platform as your platform?

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:26.530
Were there particular features
or tools or platforms or things

00:15:26.530 --> 00:15:28.870
that made your life easier
for what you were building?

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:31.270
CRAIG GANSSLE: So I have
three definitive reasons

00:15:31.270 --> 00:15:36.640
for that, the first one being
we actually started in AWS

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:39.640
and it was not
user-friendly to work with,

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:42.410
and it crashed on us
a couple of times.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.550
So we moved over to
Google Cloud Platform.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:49.810
The interface is just far easier
to use and navigate through.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:51.790
The connective
framework of things

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:54.160
like Kubernetes and Firebase
and the other things

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:58.390
that kind of plug in to GCP
were a whole lot easier to use.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.760
And Basecamp Networks,
as a company,

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:03.850
has a longstanding
partnership with Google,

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:06.880
with the Glass Development
Team for Google Glass.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:10.370
We're a G-suite
reseller and integrator.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.110
So those kinds of things--

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:15.132
it just made sense to just keep
it all in the Google house.

00:16:15.132 --> 00:16:16.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:20.470
I'm wondering, since you cannot
share the magic about going

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:24.850
from thousands of images for
training to going to 40 to 60--

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:28.770
which is amazing, and I will ask
you once we're done recording--

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:33.310
could you share any other best
practices or pieces of advice

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.970
for other companies that might
be considering doing something

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:36.970
similar to what you did?

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:37.803
CRAIG GANSSLE: Sure.

00:16:37.803 --> 00:16:42.970
Our Chief Solutions Architect,
Dr. Christian Kennedy,

00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.610
he has a really good way
of explaining to people.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.190
This is how we're explaining
the machine learning.

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:49.582
You're training a machine.

00:16:49.582 --> 00:16:51.790
And so that data-- whatever
you're training it with--

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:53.260
is coming from somewhere.

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:57.610
In our situation, it's coming
from the expert knowledge

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:01.390
of pathologists and
entomologists in agriculture

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:06.339
and how they see pathogens
and pests affecting crops.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.170
So as we train the
machine, we have

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:11.800
to go off of the best knowledge
that we get from them.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:15.097
The methods of how
we're taking pictures--

00:17:15.097 --> 00:17:16.930
and part of that reticle
design to make sure

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:18.846
that we're getting good
pictures-- making sure

00:17:18.846 --> 00:17:20.819
that they are properly
tagged, they're

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:24.790
properly classified so that
our classifiers have the best

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:29.620
accuracy possible going in to
the original scan of training

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:31.711
that machine from
the very first time.

00:17:31.711 --> 00:17:34.210
And I think that's really a big
key with any kind of machine

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:36.170
learning and even deep learning.

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:40.180
So sure, we're doing that "if
this, then that" component

00:17:40.180 --> 00:17:42.160
with machine learning
in agriculture.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:46.240
If you see this, then
it's corn leaf blight.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:47.860
But then what do you do?

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:50.650
Then we get into the deep
learning of what is next,

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:55.630
and this is what you should
do from a spring in herbicide

00:17:55.630 --> 00:17:57.160
or a pesticide.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.170
If we catch things
earlier, we're

00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:01.840
able to spray less
herbicides and pesticides.

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.420
That means the farmer can
have better crop outcome.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:07.360
That means globally, we
can impact climate change,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.240
because we're spraying
less in all of our farmland

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:11.260
around the world.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:15.070
But all of this comes
from getting great data

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:16.120
to start with.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:20.170
And so that's why intelliSCOUT,
we did not start by putting

00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:24.130
this in the hands of just all
the farmers as a $5.00 app you

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:27.190
could download and subscribe
to, but more starting with

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:30.430
the large corporations
who have the pathologists,

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:34.000
the PhD talent in-house to give
us really good data to start

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:37.060
with so that we know
we're training the machine

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:40.120
at the most accurate levels of
severity and getting the best

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:43.570
possible classifiers for
identifying what we have

00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:45.650
and what we want the
machine to recognize.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:49.240
So it's all about really
good data to start with.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:52.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
keeping on the data topic,

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:54.670
what kind of training
have you been doing?

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:58.210
Do you train the
models regularly?

00:18:58.210 --> 00:19:00.850
Did you train them once,
and they're good enough now?

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:04.060
I guess that you keep on
getting more and more images.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.070
Do you also use those
images that people

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:10.750
are using to detect if there
is some illness in their crops

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:12.370
to improve your own predictions?

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:13.453
CRAIG GANSSLE: Absolutely.

00:19:13.453 --> 00:19:15.790
So that's kind of the big
factor with TensorFlow

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:18.280
is the more you use it, the
smarter it's going to get.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:23.410
So we kind of train everything
once and really get it going.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:27.880
And the more that it gets used,
the more it's getting trained.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:30.730
And so it's getting smarter
the more it gets used.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:33.670
Because even if somebody
takes an image and that image,

00:19:33.670 --> 00:19:38.350
they want to have it classified
or have it identified, what

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:41.810
am I looking at here, that image
is also then repurposed and put

00:19:41.810 --> 00:19:43.450
into the system as a classifier.

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:44.408
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:19:44.408 --> 00:19:46.480
So one last question
on the same topic,

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:48.190
because I'm very curious--

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:51.700
so what happens if
someone takes an image

00:19:51.700 --> 00:19:54.400
and you're not able
to classify correctly?

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:55.340
Does that happen?

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.610
Do you have some
experts in-house?

00:19:57.610 --> 00:19:59.710
What is the flow in those cases?

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:02.470
CRAIG GANSSLE: It sort of
casts it off and alerts us

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:03.890
that we have an anomaly.

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.830
We have a situation
here with an image

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:08.800
that is not fitting
with the classifiers

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:10.490
that we've programmed
in the machine.

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:15.700
So if you're out scouting
corn for armyworm

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:19.120
and you take a picture
of southern rust,

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:21.430
the system's going to
say, that's not armyworm,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.590
but I don't know
what it is, we need

00:20:23.590 --> 00:20:25.340
to take a look at this image.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:28.840
Then once we program and
train it on southern rust,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:30.600
it will say, that's
not armyworm,

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:32.750
but that's southern rust.

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:35.320
So if it's something
new, it's going

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:38.500
to cast it off and alert
through the system and say,

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.760
it's not armyworm, but
I don't know what it is.

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:41.770
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:20:41.770 --> 00:20:45.670
So now I'm also super curious,
from a practical level,

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:49.190
what has been the biggest
change, in regards to how

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:53.190
agricultural farming that
you've had under this program

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:55.440
has changed from what it was
previously, if that makes

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:55.940
sense?

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:58.270
CRAIG GANSSLE: How have we
impacted farming kind of?

00:20:58.270 --> 00:20:59.395
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:20:59.395 --> 00:21:00.070
Exactly.

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.960
CRAIG GANSSLE: Going
back to whether it's

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.320
the app on a mobile device
or utilizing Google Glass,

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:08.340
part of what we want to be
able to do with intelliSCOUT,

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:10.300
as it gets smarter
and smarter and gains

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.730
more knowledge and more
crops and different diseases

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:15.579
and different pests, is
really unable smallholders

00:21:15.579 --> 00:21:16.120
of the world.

00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:18.490
We want to be able to
take this technology

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:22.100
and share it with people
in other countries--

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:26.170
so for example, in some
far remote areas of Africa

00:21:26.170 --> 00:21:28.570
where their land is
not as rich as maybe it

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:29.890
is here in the United States.

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:34.870
And so every square acre of
land is very valuable to them.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:37.480
And they want to
maximize what they

00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:40.340
can grow off of that land
to its fullest potential.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:44.290
So by being able to catch
diseases or pests far earlier

00:21:44.290 --> 00:21:46.360
using intelliSCOUT--
by sending them

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:49.300
something like a pair of
Google Glass and they put it on

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:51.490
and they launch the
intelliSCOUT application

00:21:51.490 --> 00:21:54.220
and they simply scan the
field and take pictures

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:58.000
and the results come back
in near real time with,

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.520
here's what's going on, and
then into that deep learning of,

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:02.440
here's what you need to do.

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:05.950
And so if you look at
something that would normally

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:08.839
take weeks or a month,
until you get those results,

00:22:08.839 --> 00:22:10.630
depending on weather
patterns, your disease

00:22:10.630 --> 00:22:12.070
could spread far more.

00:22:12.070 --> 00:22:14.500
And back where it
originally started,

00:22:14.500 --> 00:22:17.900
that could be just dead, gone,
and lost crop and lost yield.

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:20.270
So if we can capture
things in more real time,

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:23.590
then we can enable smallholders
with better knowledge.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.800
They're able to grow
more, grow better food,

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.580
become much more sustainable.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:31.420
So that's one way is
to just kind of enable

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:33.340
the education of
smallholders around the world

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:35.110
with better technology.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.600
The other piece of
it is just, as I

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:41.370
say with the image library,
there wasn't anything for us

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:42.250
to start with.

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:45.190
And so we're building
that catalog library now

00:22:45.190 --> 00:22:46.482
that will exist in agriculture.

00:22:46.482 --> 00:22:48.690
MARK MANDEL: So you said
something really interesting

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:51.220
there that I think you kind
of slipped in, which was,

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:54.730
once you've got a diagnosis,
giving suggestions

00:22:54.730 --> 00:22:55.822
on what people should do.

00:22:55.822 --> 00:22:57.280
Is that something
you're doing now,

00:22:57.280 --> 00:22:58.730
or is that a plan
for the future?

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:00.188
CRAIG GANSSLE: It's
something we're

00:23:00.188 --> 00:23:03.070
doing now to a small extent.

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:08.230
We want to correlate even more
data from other data points

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:09.220
on the farm.

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:11.786
So intelliSCOUT
would be-- wherever

00:23:11.786 --> 00:23:13.660
you're standing when
you take a field report,

00:23:13.660 --> 00:23:17.140
it tells you where you were
and what the weather was

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:18.860
when you took that report.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:22.810
So if you're standing in a field
and you take a picture of corn

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:25.040
and you're looking at
a particular disease,

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.820
we want to correlate data
that came from the machinery

00:23:27.820 --> 00:23:29.050
from the day it was planted.

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:32.910
So the John Deere planter or
the AGCO planter or whatever--

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:34.480
when intelliSCOUT
takes the report,

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.780
it says, yeah, but I want to go
further and see when this was

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:41.260
planted, what was the spacing,
what was the compaction,

00:23:41.260 --> 00:23:44.440
and from then till now, what's
that irrigation been like--

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:48.820
have we had a lot of rain, have
we had to manually irrigate

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:53.560
through pivots or other methods,
what's the nitrogen levels been

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:55.240
from then till now--

00:23:55.240 --> 00:23:57.760
and really start pulling
data from other data

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:00.250
points on the farm
to make intelliSCOUT

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:02.500
smarter and smarter and get
into that deep learning.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:05.390
That then allows us to get
into protective modeling.

00:24:05.390 --> 00:24:07.960
And predictive modeling
could potentially

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:09.640
change the game for agriculture.

